{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: full N3 starter design at 6c7b42d0d4c952359d87cb902aa6f7ef26a0f77d.\n\nBinding and scope\nThis is the ordinary assigned review of original request f3299ab4b2bf553f2a221a353a7cb75f04cbd5c6, builder promise 779cf3a62b080b9e34e0f4568e9489fa0ec4e753, invitation cdcdabb5bf9d83e6a66d660cf7a136898d97c798 and my accepted promise 564563d589e79483c8dae9dbcf39a64d2f2ae0fc. Primary and sole subject f3c9cb58eb8142b45d15377c4c1fd2dac1cd8b56: notes/2026-10-04-starter-specification.md. The original commission owes a Git design artifact; this is assigned design delivery, not evidence-only and not runtime delivery. Implementation is separately commissioned after exact design review/adoption.\n\nI independently read every byte of the complete 735-line, 42,599-byte note, the full invitation and original request/conditions. Raw event commit/path and sole builder-promise basis were checked. Blob fd9b977a3a25aa25dc906fec2302ed312c2660aa; UTF-8 SHA-256 93a476a0d3df63fc3fcaa53f7263f76f9a0fcc57a9dd8e02be7effc6bedfac8d. The registered detached review checkout is at that exact head and clean. One parallel static auditor independently read all 735 lines and examined public interfaces. I checked the material findings against my own document and source reads; delegated findings do not substitute for root review.\n\nThree P2 design corrections\n\n1. Supply the public Git read route or mark the coding-review and clone steps dependent on its delivery.\nEvidence: sections 5.2 (425–433), 8.4 (565–570), 10 (643). The outside reviewer receives a client-custody invitation and team membership, then uses artroom review. Section 10 says that bounded proposal reads are not designed and the starter reviewer instead reads the pushed commit with git. That is not a specified access route. At pinned d691e6e9, requests.ts127–145 permits workspace/token retrieval only to the lane holder; reads.ts113–133 dispatches proposal metadata and other Room read queries, with no Git objects or credential route; CLI main.ts995–1029 reads proposal metadata and submits the review. It does not fetch source or obtain a Git read credential. The separate canonical log clone in the smoke similarly lacks a named credential. Historical e6e67828 notes/deploy-spike.md85–90 and119 explicitly used the owner's OAuth and a read token; that historical smoke is not an outsider credential seam. N1's exact 75260d65 design note39–57 separately identifies the nonholder read gap. Public founding in a namespace does not establish anonymous Git readability.\nImpact: a cold outsider could follow every named step and have authority to approve while lacking the proposed source to judge or access for the promised fresh log clone. This defeats the useful independently reviewed coding interaction and external verification witness.\nRequested correction: specify supported, correctly scoped read acquisition/provisioning for the proposal's exact head and the published log, including the owner, public API/package/deployment availability and credential custody. If it is not delivered, identify the relevant read dependency and mark affected S6/S7/smoke portions blocked; retain the entire acceptance condition. Proposal object reads and log read access may be different seams. Do not silently share a holder's write token or presume operator OAuth, and do not call a metadata-only approval independent source review. This review does not adopt new disclosure authority or require a new client export; any necessary new choice follows its owned design/adoption lane. Confidence high; design correction small, implementation effort belongs to the read owner; correction risk low.\n\n2. Name a deliberate two-actor conflict in the focused scenarios.\nEvidence: section 1 item5 (50–51) and table8.3 (549–554). The original commissioning explicitly calls for two-actor refusal/conflict/replay. The six scenarios cover useful path, nonholder refusal, stale binding, unchanged grants, signed retry and concurrent comments. Scenario6 accepts both comments and checks their common order. That shows serialization, not a conflict. The conditional scope-overlap UI in section3.2 (203–205) is not a named witness that causes one.\nImpact: an implementation may pass every listed scenario without exposing or resolving conflicting work between actors, leaving a complete acceptance condition open.\nRequested correction: include one deliberate conflict using supported existing steps, for example overlapping live scopes with two eligible actors, or a supported generation/lease race. Specify the setup, permissions, conflict result and recorded/unrecorded outcome as the applicable protocol defines it, what the second actor sees and the history/decision path. Reuse or extend the existing focused set; retain the ordering witness if useful. No new conflict mode, stage4 feature, platform permutation suite or numerical test quota is requested. Confidence high; effort small; risk low.\n\n3. Give the starter-owned bearer/context store guarantees an explicit focused witness.\nEvidence: section7 (464–500) says the scenarios check its rules; rules2,4,5 specify the same bearer session and room, context-qualified settlement and an unresolved state when a token is unavailable. Scenario5 (553) names only signed Pat restart/resubmission. No listed scenario identifies a bearer restart under the same saved session, refusal of a replacement session/context, or a matching idempotency string from another room/actor that must not settle the operation. At d691e6e9 client room.ts538–548, unsigned replay checks that a bearer handle exists and sends through that handle; it does not establish the starter's saved session identity on the application's behalf. Public PreparedAct128–141 also does not carry that unsigned session/room context, which the proposed store correctly owns.\nImpact: the planned implementation could pass its named witnesses while replaying unsigned intent with a different session or settling the wrong stored operation. This is missing design coverage, not a claim that unbuilt code already has that bug.\nRequested correction: explicitly assign a small starter-owned witness for restart with the exact saved bearer context, and for lost/revoked/expired or replacement-session handling that leaves the operation unresolved and dispatches no substituted act. Name a focused cross-context settlement check with the same idempotency string and differing room/actor. These can share fixtures with the signed retry witness and use public interfaces; local store qualification can be distinguished from hosted token/admission behavior. State what each shows. Keep no signing keys in the store, no reconstruction of uncertain retries, no automatic new idempotency key and no substituted signer. If any witness is deferred, identify its implementation owner and dependency and qualify the claim that section7 is covered; retain the required safety semantics. No repeated client/platform suite or per-field sweeps. Confidence high; effort small; risk low.\n\nClarifications for the revision\nThe answer/proposal fields of type act are entry-ID-shaped references. At pinned schema.ts451–475, act validates format; the release handler admission.ts1573–1603 checks the thread/holder/lease/policy and does not prove that accept.answer is an answer belonging to this question. Explain that acceptance records the holder's assertion; have the page qualify a missing/wrong-question reference rather than imply a Room-proven relationship. The ordinary valid-answer selection may be checked by the application. This is a clarification of the reference example, not a demand for N2 lifecycle machinery or a new universal guard.\n\nSections8.1 and8.4 also need consistent run accounting: the designated room is founded once and reused, whereas the smoke text says each run leaves one room and repository. Say whether the smoke reuses setup or provisions a separately configured fixture and report cumulative durable state and cost accordingly. Recording charges alone does not set a work budget: name the bounded run/input/read scope and how growing retained history or a budget refusal is qualified. Do not erase provenance or introduce an unsupported cleanup/delete route. Retention/cleanup remains an operator dependency. Preserve dated inventory claims as historical; current N1 review and release approvals do not retroactively change what this frozen note observed.\n\nArchitecture conclusion\nThe separate repository, six pinned public packages and lock integrity, absence of source imports/workspace links/platform patches, one public-client coding and actual Jam path, and separate implementation lanes S1–S7 are a coherent design. The four acts use supported candidate shapes: open with workspace, anchored comment, holder-only release and take; current holder acceptance and lease-expiry takeover are deliberate reference-example choices. Full addressed-work lifecycle stays N2's. The proposed declaration update correctly changes ask binding without implicitly changing answer/accept/resume; historical labels and grants stay sequence-qualified. The shared client preparation seam is reused rather than replaced with an application signer. Main/integration/package/deployment/registry/owner dependencies are explicitly identified. The public read gap must be resolved or explicitly made blocking before the cold useful coding path can run. No platform/protocol authority is adopted by this verdict.\n\nSecurity conclusion\nDevelopment files and a loopback server hold actor keys; the browser receives no Room credentials. Origin checks and a per-start local capability are proposed and require later source verification. The separate recovery key is unregistered and dev-only same-host custody is disclosed; real recovery remains offline. Three seeded keys do not demonstrate independent judgment. Cold acceptance requires a separately controlled reviewer and pinned command. Saving exact founding state before found and exact prepared context before dispatch is sound; same-session replay and context settlement belong to the starter and need the named witnesses above. No new security defect was established by the scoped static source reads. No provider, token, Git credential, browser or runtime behavior was probed here.\n\nSimplification conclusion\nKeep the static page, one custody model, one file store boundary, app-owned public-interface conformance, published policy evaluator and one justified external smoke. Do not create a private test SDK, duplicate signing, add cross-origin support, or infer first-Jam gates. Three focused corrections can be specified through existing seams and owned dependencies; they do not constrain the complete functionality with a schedule estimate.\n\nFull acceptance and scope retained\nBoth builder tarball run and true registry cold run remain owed; handed tarballs are qualified evidence. Exact package versions/integrities, deployed configuration, commands, elapsed time, manual help, actor control, durable state and cost must be recorded. A coding interaction and one actual supported Jam interaction at a pinned Jam revision must use the same client. Public fixtures or app-owned public-interface scenarios cannot import private Cloudflare test internals. Local policy evaluation is not hosted admission, Git, publication, sandbox or MCP proof. The hosted machine checker is explicitly absent, independent-member review is the chosen route, and published-log verification cannot prove instantaneous publication completeness. C3 trusted agent and C5 browser adapters stay separately owned. S1–S7 and N1/N5/registry/public-repository/retention/provisioning dependencies remain complete. A later exact implementation head needs ordinary independent review and its authorized focused checks; this design verdict supplies none of those runtime results.\n\nMethod and limitations\nFull frozen document review, raw provenance/blob/SHA identity checks, ordinary assigned prepare and selected static source comparisons at the note's own pins. No tests, build, typecheck, install, repository code execution, private diagnostic, provider call, publication, deployment or external-person communication. All commands in the note remain specifications. I did not perform a new whole-platform runtime/source audit or substitute historical observations for this head. Evidence retained under /tmp/artroom-checker-n3-6c7b-4bRTUV.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f3c9cb58eb8142b45d15377c4c1fd2dac1cd8b56","binding":"assigned","head":"6c7b42d0d4c952359d87cb902aa6f7ef26a0f77d","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:564563d589e79483c8dae9dbcf39a64d2f2ae0fc\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:779cf3a62b080b9e34e0f4568e9489fa0ec4e753\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8e50045178348b517f0c053ab4b5eacb0653634e","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}